6743" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175794 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/c2/c2261af6cde7c606ab1fd25ac17de587680089fd42974290e06ab9ac397eace7.svg)\n\nGiven that $\\lambda = 3$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ and that this eigenvalue has corresponding generalized eigenvectors of rank $2,$ calculate one of these generalized eigenvectors.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-3I)^{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175794 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b5fa2c26-75c8-5654-9c04-e6fa9aa3c08a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175794 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $k$ is a vector $\\mathbf{v}$ that satisfies \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I)^k \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}\n\\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad\n(A - \\lambda I)^{k-1} \\mathbf{v} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nTo find a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda = 3,$ we need to find a vector $\\mathbf v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A -3I)^2 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}\n\\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad\n(A -3I) \\mathbf{v} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each condition:\n\n- We start by solving $(A -3I)^2 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}.$ We're given that\n![](images/32/321f4692e18dec20972798388b22c0b42d62220f38d04494229f379ddeaed1fe.svg)\nConverting this matrix to reduced row echelon form, we get\n![](images/8c/8c733431226bd217b6d7821966a2cd6405b25c833a55f0b9b51ad2d77df8890e.svg)\nThis means that the solution to $(A-3I)^2 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ is $v_2 = 0,$ where $v_1$ and $v_3$ are free variables. Therefore, the solution to $(A -3I)^2 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={v}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $(A -3I) \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}.$ We have\n![](images/c3/c33f1972e1168986d60a95e350ff0d40c62995352352901702dc682c8cd7111f.svg)\nConverting this matrix to reduced row echelon form, we get\n![](images/91/914619ae119ac94a17e7b889e58cd64e9acc47534d0251505018a1f59486d6c8.svg)\nThis means that the solution to $(A -3I) \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ is $v_2 = v_3 = 0,$ where $v_1$ is a free variable. Therefore, the solution to $(A -3I) \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={v}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing our two solutions, we see that the vector ![](images/87/8784c55b0d490631a62d16f0c74d1ecf6935348e9063c7346d9032fca3659a61.svg) satisfies $(A -3I)^2 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ but does *not* satisfy $(A -3 I) \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}.$  \n\nHence, ![](images/5c/5cdb1cf5af8b5e70246d471125081eb75454e9bfcd2e6013de4ab16c166ac851.svg) is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ of $A.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175836 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9058" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175836 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/92/925b1deaec3a2ab84d792e0933841de1c161519810dc63c90c219fdddec24c88.svg)\n\nGiven that $\\lambda = 3$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ and that this eigenvalue has corresponding generalized eigenvectors of rank $3,$ calculate one of these eigenvectors.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results:*\n\n![](images/42/42ea30dd612a219c3d3d65646332097691907496a666a62551c81cd3107f04a4.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175836 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "38269e05-68a8-5c04-91a4-49efe5d327cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175836 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $k$ is a vector $\\mathbf{v}$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I)^k \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}\n\\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad\n(A - \\lambda I)^{k-1} \\mathbf{v} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nTo find a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to $\\lambda = 3,$ we need to find a vector $\\mathbf v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A - 3I)^3 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}\n\\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad\n(A - 3I)^2 \\mathbf{v} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each condition:\n\n- We start by solving $(A -3I)^3 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}.$ We're given that\n![](images/c1/c1d460af9660f65349be7b5e0367e1fdc45c50718bde5628813aeb6527335a30.svg)\nConverting this matrix to reduced row echelon form, we get\n![](images/b0/b084ecf755cf1ce518655a951121eca41d0a68417b1d9c798cb980b69e2bed69.svg)\nThis means the solution to $(A -3I)^3 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ is $v_4 = 0,$ where $v_1,v_2,$ and $v_3$ are free variables. Therefore, the solution to $(A-3I)^3 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3} \\\\ {v}_{4}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={v}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $(A -3I)^2 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}.$ We're given that\n![](images/bc/bca82b51d2615a9044e3648b5b58efe51a032f27c4372fcbd0cd8160912b64c7.svg)\nConverting this matrix to reduced row echelon form, we get\n![](images/d0/d08d1db901943703ea2d504658c42213f804db2dc8f79af36df5960f404a2d08.svg)\nThis means the solution to $(A -3I)^2 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ is $v_3 = v_4 = 0,$ where $v_1$ and $v_2$ are free variables. So, the solution to $(A-3I)^2 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3} \\\\ {v}_{4}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={v}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing our two solutions, we see that the vector\n![](images/8e/8ee5d50977be2e9bed358aaa6e3ddd69f066d1ed20b9cff66f47cb6dd5a94187.svg)\nsatisfies $(A -3I)^3 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ but does *not* satisfy $(A -3 I)^2 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}.$ \n\nHence, ![](images/7e/7e12c9e6f43a4d269169b3bee86baf9908804f6ce42ba1b4a75d4cfe3a3ed9b1.svg) is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ of $A.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175837 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9059" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175837 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/ca/ca890e668f8a1e66488bf8670fc32cc6b1f78d523f6f84c066e6aa3c854fd7a4.svg)\n\nGiven that $\\lambda = 4$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ and that this eigenvalue has corresponding generalized eigenvectors of rank $2,$ calculate one of these generalized eigenvectors." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175837 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6cf182b1-cb9a-5adf-b40a-cc2f578b8d29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175837 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $k$ is a vector $\\mathbf{v}$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I)^k \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}\n\\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad\n(A - \\lambda I)^{k-1} \\mathbf{v} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nTo find a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda = 4,$ we need to find a vector $\\mathbf v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A -4I)^2 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}\n\\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad\n(A -4I) \\mathbf{v} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each condition:\n\n- We start by solving $(A -4I)^2 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}.$ First, we have\n![](images/39/39ab14ea9e366cc8ac9253e4834b64f271b302e2fe6ff9de1ea5c583769904e2.svg)\nComputing its square, we see that this matrix is nilpotent:\n![](images/0d/0d98e89e38f42c7c0cbd66776260f593f4722c005c35df2caac5dd1d9b4de3ff.svg)\nThis means that *any* vector in $\\mathbb R^2$ is a solution to $(A -4I)^2 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}.$ Therefore, the solution to $(A -4I)^2 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2}\\end{aligned}]={v}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $(A -4I) \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}.$ We have\n![](images/39/39d77e503367d056ace2d621f4fadc632c6147011d64643c1751368ab1c28cfa.svg)\nConverting this matrix to reduced row echelon form, we get\n![](images/c4/c4298b02c6b1c9ce208f68da7eb64879395d7a4313616e9108a6fffe18980e13.svg)\nThis means that the solution to $(A -4I) \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ is $v_1 = 0,$ where $v_2$ is a free variable. Therefore, the solution to $(A -4I) \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\end{aligned}]={v}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing our two solutions, we see that the vector ![](images/2c/2c499603c0dd08f8b3720f277a7f368d5ac77ecba0b131c547f031a5f2600f57.svg)satisfies $(A -4I)^2 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ but does *not* satisfy $(A -4 I) \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}.$  \n\nHence, ![](images/5f/5f9b615aa97947c56d96da8ccd5df0e5aec9c5d34ef07d54e8b78395f38c9d3f.svg) is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ of $A.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175945 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175945 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176572 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175945 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175945 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186367019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175945 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186368744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175945 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186380559 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175945 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186381097 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186