 see that the vector\n\n![](images/cf/cff85d0846c6f53028f4cfae1b3d17feb79c0cac36a92addeb88393f6430df97.svg)\n\nsatisfies $(A +3I)^2 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ but does *not* satisfy $(A +3 I) \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}.$ \n\nHence, ![](images/5b/5b9968cb8a6950c87d94bdf617cb6d297ddfeac315805f68008dfd7fff0fa923.svg) is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ of $A.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176996 #edb/ref 1065 5423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176996 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e3b0674e-a304-5e37-b45a-b512efd3ba3a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176996 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367012 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186367013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367012 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109717" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367012 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367012 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5$ for the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367012 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "59ee41c2-95f3-577e-8ef1-c85d597cce44" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367012 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda ,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $k$ is a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)^{k}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-\\lambda I)^{k-1}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTo find a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-5,$ we need to find a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A+5I)^{2}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A+5I)v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each condition:\n\n- We start by solving $(A+5I)^{2}v=0.$ First, we have\n\n$$\nA+5I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nComputing its square, we see that this matrix is nilpotent:\n\n$$\n(A+5I)^{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis means that *any* vector in ${R}^{2}$ is a solution to $(A+5I)^{2}v=0.$ Therefore, the